# mangame reads a .env from the working directory, from beside the executable,
# from your configuration directory, or from wherever MANGAME_ENV_FILE points.
# Copy this file to ".env" and uncomment what you need.
#
# Put it in the configuration directory if you want it to apply to the copy
# that starts at login, which has no working directory worth speaking of:
#   Linux    ~/.config/mangame/.env
#   macOS    ~/Library/Application Support/mangame/.env
#   Windows  %APPDATA%\mangame\.env
#
# .env is git-ignored on purpose. Nothing about your setup — which manga you
# follow, which artwork you imported, where you keep it — belongs in the
# repository. A fresh clone tracks nothing and ships no artwork but its own.
#
# A variable that is already set in the real environment always wins over this
# file, so a launcher or a one-off "MANGAME_HOME=/tmp/x mangame" still works.
#
# Syntax: KEY=value, one per line. '#' starts a comment. Quote a value to keep
# spaces or a literal '#'. There is no variable interpolation.

# Settings, database and artwork in one directory instead of the platform's
# usual location. A portable install on a USB stick, or a scratch copy that
# leaves your real setup alone while you try something out.
#MANGAME_HOME=./local

# Imported artwork somewhere of its own, wherever the rest lives. Worth
# separating: it is bulky, it is the part most likely to be collected by hand,
# and it outlives a reinstall that throws the database away.
#MANGAME_EMBLEM_DIR=~/Pictures/mangame-emblems
